/* Ninety-six articles about a user interface, with two pictures between them.
 *
 * The block model has carried `image` and `video` since media blocks landed —
 * a typed union, an embed-host allowlist, `kb_embeddable` in the database and
 * `embeddable` beside it, a renderer shared by the full knowledge base and the
 * contextual help panel. All of it built, and one article on file using an
 * image and one using a video. A capability nothing exercises is the same kind
 * of claim as a sort order nothing writes.
 *
 * Worse than the absence was the presence. The one article that did carry a
 * picture carried a stock photograph from a photo library, with alt text
 * reading "The settlement runs screen, with the period list on the left and the
 * Approve button at the end of each row." It is not that screen. A reader who
 * cannot see the image is told there is a button in a place, and a reader who
 * can see it is shown somebody else's desk. That is worse than no picture at
 * all, and it is removed here rather than joined by eighty more like it.
 *
 * The pictures are of the screens themselves: every screen in all four
 * consoles, captured through the ordinary sign-in as the persona that reads the
 * article, and served from the `kb-assets` bucket this marketplace already
 * owns. Eighty-two screens, eighty-four articles — two screens carry two
 * articles each, which is why the counts differ.
 *
 * The videos are placeholders and say so in their own captions. What is real
 * about them is the plumbing: they are rotated across all four URL forms the
 * allowlist accepts — a Vimeo player URL, a Vimeo watch page, a YouTube watch
 * page and a youtu.be short link — because `embedUrl` has a conversion branch
 * for each and until now no article on file exercised any of them. A recorded
 * walkthrough replaces the URL in place and nothing else has to change.
 *
 * A walkthrough goes on the articles that walk you through doing something —
 * `howto`, `start` and `fix`. A `concept` or a `policy` article has nothing to
 * watch, and a video on one is a play button that wastes the reader's time.
 *
 * Rebuilt rather than appended to, so running this twice produces what running
 * it once did: the prose is kept in its order, the picture goes second where
 * a reader is oriented by it, and the walkthrough goes last.
 */

create temporary table kb_media (id text primary key, img jsonb, vid jsonb) on commit drop;

